                <Note>
                    <NoteId>0</NoteId>
                    <NoteColor>0xf9ec72ff</NoteColor>
                    <NoteContent>Example #1: Two versions of the same customer class. CUSTOMER-1 calculates the values of the derivable attributes age, customerYears, and loyalty level (loyalty level is assumed to depend on the number of customer years). We can easily see how instances of CUSTOMER-2 contain wrong slot values for Harold Eastman. This conceptual error cannot be detected automatically, but may lead to customer dissatisfaction.</NoteContent>
                    <NotePosition>
                        <XPosition>777.0</XPosition>
                        <YPosition>253.0</YPosition>
                    </NotePosition>
                </Note>
                <Note>
                    <NoteId>1</NoteId>
                    <NoteColor>0xf9ec72ff</NoteColor>
                    <NoteContent>Example #2: Plays of songs and artists. This model captures when a user listens to a song. Three operations are added that automatically return the duration display time, the number of listens for each song, and the number of listens for each artist.</NoteContent>
                    <NotePosition>
                        <XPosition>1759.0</XPosition>
                        <YPosition>309.0</YPosition>
                    </NotePosition>
                </Note>
